
# Ambient Memory Layer — MVP Engineering Spec

## 0. Product Boundary (READ FIRST)

This product is:
- NOT a writing tool
- NOT a notes app
- NOT a workspace

Users always type and work in OTHER products.
We only appear:
- to capture
- to recall
- to disappear

If a feature requires users to “work inside” our app, it is out of scope.

---

## 1. Core Data Model

### 1.1 Event (Primary Primitive)

An Event represents something that passed through user attention.

Required fields:
- event_id (UUID)
- type (link | text | screenshot | thought)
- payload (raw content)
- source:
  - url (if available)
  - page_title (if available)
  - app_name (if available)
- created_at (timestamp)

Derived (internal only):
- last_accessed_at
- reuse_count
- dismiss_count
- importance_score (internal, invisible)
- decay_score (internal, invisible)

STRICT RULES:
- No titles
- No tags
- No folders
- No user-defined metadata

---

## 2. Feature Specs

---

## 2.1 Zero-Friction Event Capture

### Goal
Capture anything with ONE action and ZERO decisions.

### Surfaces
- Chrome Extension
- Global Hotkey
- Share Sheet (optional later)

### Triggers
- User clicks “Save”
- User presses hotkey (Cmd + Shift + S)

### System Input
- Raw payload (URL, selected text, screenshot, or typed text)
- Timestamp
- Source context

### System Behavior
1. Create new Event
2. Append to event store
3. Show optional toast: “Saved”
4. Return control immediately

### Explicit Non-Goals
- No title prompt
- No classification
- No editor
- No navigation

### Acceptance Criteria
- Capture completes in <300ms
- User can capture without leaving current app
- Capture never asks a question

---

## 2.2 Append-Only Stream (Read-Only)

### Goal
Provide a reliable memory log without maintenance burden.

### Surface
- Extension panel or minimal web view

### Behavior
- Display events in strict chronological order
- Newest first
- Infinite scroll

Each event shows:
- Payload preview
- Source
- Timestamp

### Explicit Non-Goals
- No states (processed/unprocessed)
- No filters

### Acceptance Criteria
- User can scroll entire history
- User never needs to “organize” anything

---

## 2.3 Behavioral Signals Engine

### Goal
Infer importance without user input.

### Signals to Track (ONLY THESE)

Positive signals:
- Event opened
- Text copied from event
- Event selected after recall
- Event reused multiple times

Negative signals:
- Event dismissed during recall
- Event ignored repeatedly
- Time since last access

### Signals NOT Allowed
- Keystroke logging
- Full document reading
- Continuous typing observation

### System Behavior
- Update importance_score
- Update decay_score
- No UI exposure

### Acceptance Criteria
- No settings
- No dashboards
- No user awareness required

---

## 2.4 Earned Recall (Explicit Trigger Only)

### Goal
Surface relevant past events during thinking moments.

### Where Recall Happens
- Browser text fields
- Docs, Notion, Slack, Email, VS Code
- Any page via extension

### Trigger (MVP: ONE ONLY)
- User presses recall hotkey (Cmd + Shift + R)

### Context Passed
ONE of:
- Selected text
- Clipboard contents
- Current page URL

### System Behavior
1. Fetch candidate events
2. Rank by:
   - Context similarity
   - Recency
   - importance_score
3. Select MAX 2 events
4. Show in side panel or overlay

### Copy Rules
Allowed:
> “You’ve seen something like this before”

Not allowed:
- “Insight”
- “AI recommends”
- “Based on your knowledge graph”

### Dismissal Behavior
- User can dismiss each item
- Dismissal reduces future resurfacing probability

### Acceptance Criteria
- Recall never blocks typing
- Recall never opens automatically
- Recall always disappears cleanly

---

## 2.5 Working Set (Internal Only)

### Goal
Reflect current cognitive focus without user management.

### Definition
A rolling internal subset of ~10–20 events weighted by:
- Recent reuse
- Contextual similarity
- Low decay_score

### Behavior
- Bias recall toward working set
- Never expose as UI

### Explicit Non-Goals
- No pinning
- No starring
- No “focus mode”

### Acceptance Criteria
- Recall quality improves over time
- User never manages focus manually
---

## 3. Privacy & Trust Constraints (Non-Negotiable)

- No continuous typing observation
- No reading full documents
- No storing user-authored content unless explicitly captured
- Recall only runs on explicit user action

Violation = feature rejection.

---

## 4.Success Metrics (Internal)

We do NOT track:
- Time spent
- Engagement frequency
- Items saved per day

We DO track:
- Recall acceptance rate
- Dismissal rate
- Repeat reuse of resurfaced events
- Long gaps with successful return

---

## 5. Kill Criteria

Stop or redesign if:
- Users ask for folders or tags
- Users feel behind after inactivity
- Recall feels random or noisy
- Capture requires explanation

---

## 6. Final Engineering Principle

If a feature:
- Requires configuration
- Requires explanation
- Requires maintenance

It is OUT OF SCOPE.

